WEBVTT

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:05.420
Imagine you're developing
a new protective coating.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.180
It needs to resist corrosion,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.450
have the right surface finish,
and remain affordable.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.460
But with different binders,
pigments, and additives,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.440
the number of possible combinations
quickly becomes enormous.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.090
You also need to choose the mixing
speed, temperature, and drying time.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.470
So which formulation should you test next?

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.510
A fixed design of experiments
gives you a structured plan.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:33.110
But that plan is decided before
the first results arrive.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.470
What if every new measurement could help
you choose a better next experiment?

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:41.400
That's the idea behind
AI-enhanced design of experiments.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.500
Instead of following a fixed
plan, we use machine learning

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.460
to make each new decision more informed.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.480
Here's how it works with STOCHOS.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.020
We start with the data you already
have: previous development results

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.870
or a small initial set of experiments,
including ingredient concentrations,

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.100
process settings,
and measured coating properties.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.549
From these measurements, machine
learning builds a surrogate model.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:07.630
It learns how the inputs relate
to the coating's properties,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.260
including complex
interactions between them.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.280
The effect of an additive, for example,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.306
may depend on the binder
or processing temperature.

00:01:15.326 --> 00:01:18.540
The model can then predict how
untested combinations are likely

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.120
to perform, without producing
and measuring every one.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.321
But predictions alone aren't enough.

00:01:24.341 --> 00:01:27.300
It also estimates uncertainty,
revealing which predictions

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.750
are well supported and where
there is still more to learn.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:34.520
Now we define our goals:
maximize corrosion protection,

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.600
minimize material cost,
and achieve a specific gloss level.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.680
We also have practical constraints, such

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.120
as viscosity limits
and ingredient concentrations.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.350
These requirements can conflict,
so we're looking for the best balance.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.250
This is where Bayesian
optimization comes in.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.960
The best predicted formulation isn't
necessarily the best next experiment.

00:01:56.301 --> 00:01:58.240
A less certain combination could offer

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.060
an even greater opportunity
for improvement.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.410
Bayesian optimization uses the model's
predictions and uncertainty to propose a

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:10.430
promising next experiment, considering
our objectives and constraints together.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.890
And because the model has learned
relationships across the inputs,

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:17.920
it can change several ingredients
and process parameters at once.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.560
We mix the suggested formulation,
test it, and measure the results.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.330
And every result teaches
the model something new,

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.910
or confirms its predictions
and lowers its uncertainty.

00:02:29.641 --> 00:02:32.011
That measurement goes back into our data.

00:02:32.031 --> 00:02:35.171
The surrogate model updates
its predictions and uncertainty,

00:02:35.191 --> 00:02:38.061
and the optimizer selects
the next experiment.

00:02:38.081 --> 00:02:41.211
Test, measure, learn, repeat.

00:02:41.831 --> 00:02:45.371
Each new result influences
where the search goes next.

00:02:45.391 --> 00:02:49.531
Instead of following a fixed sequence,
we can concentrate experimental effort

00:02:49.551 --> 00:02:53.161
on combinations with the greatest
potential for improvement.

00:02:53.181 --> 00:02:56.931
And the knowledge gained is useful
beyond the optimization itself.

00:02:56.951 --> 00:03:00.191
Through sensitivity analysis,
we can explore which ingredients

00:03:00.211 --> 00:03:02.660
and process settings have
the greatest influence

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.939
on the results, helping us
understand the process better.

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:09.071
In suitable applications,
this approach can reduce the number

00:03:09.091 --> 00:03:12.930
of physical experiments needed
to reach promising formulations.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:16.150
Fewer tests mean less
material consumption,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:20.230
fewer samples to prepare
and measure, and lower costs.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:25.121
They can also free up laboratory
capacity and shorten development cycles.

00:03:25.141 --> 00:03:29.971
With STOCHOS Flow, these steps become
a configurable, repeatable workflow.

00:03:29.991 --> 00:03:34.331
You can connect existing tools,
automate data handling and model updates,

00:03:34.351 --> 00:03:38.361
and document experiments and optimization
progress for later review.

00:03:38.381 --> 00:03:42.781
All of this runs locally on your own
hardware, using your experimental,

00:03:42.801 --> 00:03:46.061
process, and, where relevant,
simulation data.

00:03:46.081 --> 00:03:49.601
The optimization can run without
an internet connection, helping keep

00:03:49.621 --> 00:03:53.981
proprietary formulations and development
knowledge within your own environment.

00:03:54.001 --> 00:03:56.909
The goal is to make better-informed
development decisions

00:03:56.929 --> 00:04:00.941
with the data you already have
and the experiments you can afford.

00:04:00.961 --> 00:04:04.921
That's AI-enhanced design
of experiments with STOCHOS.

